1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,687
Late summer, 480 BC.

2
00:00:04,026 --> 00:00:05,977
A strip of shore between a mountain

3
00:00:05,977 --> 00:00:06,897
and the sea,

4
00:00:06,897 --> 00:00:09,449
in places no wider than a road.

5
00:00:09,449 --> 00:00:11,426
Coming down the coast toward it:

6
00:00:11,426 --> 00:00:13,597
the largest invasion force Greece had

7
00:00:13,597 --> 00:00:14,833
ever seen.

8
00:00:14,833 --> 00:00:16,261
Waiting inside it:

9
00:00:16,261 --> 00:00:18,260
about seven thousand Greeks,

10
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:20,862
and three hundred of them from Sparta.

11
00:00:21,164 --> 00:00:22,499
For two days,

12
00:00:22,499 --> 00:00:24,033
the pass held.

13
00:00:24,609 --> 00:00:26,769
Then a goat path through the mountains

14
00:00:26,769 --> 00:00:29,012
changed everything.

15
00:00:29,012 --> 00:00:30,672
By the third afternoon,

16
00:00:30,672 --> 00:00:32,842
a king of Sparta was dead,

17
00:00:32,842 --> 00:00:34,247
and the men who had stayed with

18
00:00:34,247 --> 00:00:36,046
him lay around him on a low

19
00:00:36,046 --> 00:00:36,988
hill.

20
00:00:37,244 --> 00:00:38,765
It is a harder story than the

21
00:00:38,765 --> 00:00:39,694
legend.

22
00:00:39,694 --> 00:00:41,184
And a better one.

23
00:00:41,184 --> 00:00:42,020
This

24
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:44,100
is Thermopylae.

25
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:51,116
If you want more history told this

26
00:00:51,116 --> 00:00:52,521
carefully, subscribe.

27
00:00:52,521 --> 00:00:54,230
Now, back to the pass.

28
00:00:54,767 --> 00:00:56,218
Ten years earlier,

29
00:00:56,218 --> 00:00:58,505
a Persian army had landed at Marathon,

30
00:00:58,505 --> 00:00:59,748
north of Athens,

31
00:00:59,748 --> 00:01:02,295
and been thrown back into the sea.

32
00:01:02,295 --> 00:01:03,537
The Great King,

33
00:01:03,537 --> 00:01:06,056
Darius, planned to return.

34
00:01:06,056 --> 00:01:07,529
He died first.

35
00:01:07,529 --> 00:01:10,803
His son Xerxes inherited the empire,

36
00:01:10,803 --> 00:01:13,858
the grudge, and the preparations.

37
00:01:14,374 --> 00:01:17,021
For years the Persians stockpiled grain

38
00:01:17,021 --> 00:01:18,194
along the route,

39
00:01:18,194 --> 00:01:19,796
dug a canal through the neck of

40
00:01:19,796 --> 00:01:22,130
Mount Athos so the fleet could avoid

41
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:24,467
the cape that had wrecked ships before,

42
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:26,951
and bridged the Hellespont,

43
00:01:26,951 --> 00:01:29,389
the strait between Asia and Europe,

44
00:01:29,389 --> 00:01:31,259
with two floating roads built on

45
00:01:31,259 --> 00:01:32,563
hundreds of ships.

46
00:01:32,563 --> 00:01:34,525
Herodotus says that when a storm broke

47
00:01:34,525 --> 00:01:35,814
the first bridges,

48
00:01:35,814 --> 00:01:38,114
Xerxes had the sea whipped.

49
00:01:40,569 --> 00:01:42,531
How big was the army?

50
00:01:42,531 --> 00:01:44,726
Herodotus counted more than two million

51
00:01:44,726 --> 00:01:46,165
fighting men.

52
00:01:46,165 --> 00:01:47,767
Modern historians think somewhere

53
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:49,915
between a hundred thousand and three

54
00:01:49,915 --> 00:01:52,051
hundred thousand, with a fleet of

55
00:01:52,051 --> 00:01:53,969
several hundred warships.

56
00:01:54,298 --> 00:01:56,248
Even the low figure was larger than

57
00:01:56,248 --> 00:01:58,207
anything Greece had faced.

58
00:01:58,207 --> 00:02:00,645
Heralds went ahead demanding earth and

59
00:02:00,645 --> 00:02:01,609
water:

60
00:02:01,609 --> 00:02:04,002
the sign of submission.

61
00:02:04,314 --> 00:02:06,928
Many northern cities gave it.

62
00:02:07,867 --> 00:02:10,607
The Greeks were not a nation.

63
00:02:10,607 --> 00:02:12,209
They were hundreds of quarrelling

64
00:02:12,209 --> 00:02:13,163
cities.

65
00:02:13,515 --> 00:02:16,290
In 481, the ones that chose to

66
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:19,506
resist met at Corinth and agreed,

67
00:02:19,506 --> 00:02:22,039
barely, to fight together.

68
00:02:22,308 --> 00:02:24,987
Sparta would lead on land.

69
00:02:24,987 --> 00:02:27,460
Athens, which had just built two hundred

70
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:29,852
warships with silver from its mines,

71
00:02:29,852 --> 00:02:32,024
would carry the fight at sea.

72
00:02:32,331 --> 00:02:34,636
The plan was simple.

73
00:02:34,636 --> 00:02:36,923
Block the land road into central Greece

74
00:02:36,923 --> 00:02:39,559
at Thermopylae, and block the sea beside

75
00:02:39,559 --> 00:02:40,894
it at Artemisium,

76
00:02:40,894 --> 00:02:43,030
so the two Persian forces could not

77
00:02:43,030 --> 00:02:44,738
support each other.

78
00:02:47,679 --> 00:02:49,490
But the timing was bad.

79
00:02:49,490 --> 00:02:51,452
Sparta was keeping the festival of the

80
00:02:51,452 --> 00:02:54,761
Carneia, and the Olympic truce was in

81
00:02:54,761 --> 00:02:55,553
force.

82
00:02:55,553 --> 00:02:57,954
So Sparta sent an advance guard:

83
00:02:57,954 --> 00:02:59,724
one of its two kings,

84
00:02:59,724 --> 00:03:01,989
Leonidas, with three hundred full

85
00:03:01,989 --> 00:03:04,432
citizens, each chosen,

86
00:03:04,432 --> 00:03:07,633
Herodotus says, because he had a living

87
00:03:07,633 --> 00:03:09,179
son.

88
00:03:09,179 --> 00:03:12,849
Leonidas was probably about sixty.

89
00:03:13,583 --> 00:03:14,860
On the road north,

90
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:16,462
the allies joined:

91
00:03:16,462 --> 00:03:18,889
men from Tegea and Mantinea,

92
00:03:18,889 --> 00:03:22,290
Corinth, Phlius, Mycenae,

93
00:03:22,290 --> 00:03:24,287
seven hundred from Thespiae,

94
00:03:24,287 --> 00:03:25,994
four hundred from Thebes,

95
00:03:25,994 --> 00:03:28,003
and the Phocians and Locrians,

96
00:03:28,003 --> 00:03:31,069
whose land lay just beyond the pass.

97
00:03:34,688 --> 00:03:36,719
Roughly seven thousand men,

98
00:03:36,719 --> 00:03:38,635
with their helot servants.

99
00:03:38,635 --> 00:03:40,376
They were told the main army would

100
00:03:40,376 --> 00:03:43,133
follow after the festival.

101
00:03:43,133 --> 00:03:46,755
Thermopylae means the Hot Gates.

102
00:03:46,755 --> 00:03:49,298
Sulphur springs still steam out of the

103
00:03:49,298 --> 00:03:50,310
cliff.

104
00:03:51,210 --> 00:03:52,803
In 480 BC

105
00:03:53,114 --> 00:03:54,600
the sea came right up to the

106
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:56,042
foot of the mountain.

107
00:03:56,042 --> 00:03:58,190
The pass ran for several kilometres

108
00:03:58,190 --> 00:03:59,967
between cliff and water,

109
00:03:59,967 --> 00:04:02,265
and at its narrowest points it was

110
00:04:02,265 --> 00:04:04,101
barely a cart's width.

111
00:04:04,398 --> 00:04:06,233
Today the river silt has pushed the

112
00:04:06,233 --> 00:04:08,810
coast several kilometres away,

113
00:04:08,810 --> 00:04:12,074
and the ancient shoreline is a highway.

114
00:04:12,398 --> 00:04:14,337
Across the middle of the pass stood

115
00:04:14,337 --> 00:04:17,622
an old wall the Phocians had built.

116
00:04:17,622 --> 00:04:19,480
The Greeks rebuilt it and camped behind

117
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:20,369
it.

118
00:04:20,369 --> 00:04:22,981
The men who held it were hoplites:

119
00:04:22,981 --> 00:04:24,632
citizen infantry.

120
00:04:25,067 --> 00:04:27,226
Each carried a round shield almost a

121
00:04:27,226 --> 00:04:30,883
metre across, wood faced with bronze;

122
00:04:30,883 --> 00:04:33,008
a spear of two metres or more,

123
00:04:33,008 --> 00:04:35,330
with an iron head and a bronze

124
00:04:35,330 --> 00:04:36,956
spike at the butt;

125
00:04:36,956 --> 00:04:38,233
a short sword;

126
00:04:38,233 --> 00:04:39,905
a bronze helmet;

127
00:04:39,905 --> 00:04:42,761
body armour of bronze or layered linen;

128
00:04:42,761 --> 00:04:44,362
and greaves.

129
00:04:44,362 --> 00:04:46,823
Spartans fought in crimson cloaks and

130
00:04:46,823 --> 00:04:48,785
wore their hair long.

131
00:04:48,785 --> 00:04:50,654
The famous lambda on the shield comes

132
00:04:50,654 --> 00:04:51,479
later;

133
00:04:51,479 --> 00:04:54,509
in 480 it did not exist.

134
00:04:54,509 --> 00:04:56,413
They fought in a phalanx:

135
00:04:56,413 --> 00:04:58,271
ranks of men standing shoulder to

136
00:04:58,271 --> 00:05:01,092
shoulder, each shield covering the man

137
00:05:01,092 --> 00:05:02,218
on his left,

138
00:05:02,218 --> 00:05:04,709
spears levelled over the rim.

139
00:05:04,709 --> 00:05:05,870
In the open,

140
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:08,157
numbers could flank a phalanx.

141
00:05:08,157 --> 00:05:10,607
In a corridor a few metres wide,

142
00:05:10,607 --> 00:05:12,438
there was no flank.

143
00:05:12,438 --> 00:05:13,970
The Persian infantry,

144
00:05:13,970 --> 00:05:15,898
as Herodotus describes them,

145
00:05:15,898 --> 00:05:17,372
wore felt caps,

146
00:05:17,372 --> 00:05:19,508
trousers and sleeved tunics,

147
00:05:19,508 --> 00:05:22,132
with scale armour under the cloth.

148
00:05:22,132 --> 00:05:24,013
They carried wicker shields,

149
00:05:24,013 --> 00:05:26,370
short spears, daggers,

150
00:05:26,370 --> 00:05:29,161
and above all bows.

151
00:05:29,161 --> 00:05:31,321
Their best were the ten thousand the

152
00:05:31,321 --> 00:05:33,712
Greeks called the Immortals,

153
00:05:33,712 --> 00:05:35,802
because every man who fell

154
00:05:35,802 --> 00:05:38,300
was replaced at once.

155
00:05:39,574 --> 00:05:41,072
They were brave,

156
00:05:41,072 --> 00:05:43,336
professional, and equipped for a

157
00:05:43,336 --> 00:05:45,845
different kind of war.

158
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:50,191
Xerxes camped in the plain and waited

159
00:05:50,191 --> 00:05:53,509
four days, expecting the Greeks to run.

160
00:05:53,509 --> 00:05:55,320
A horseman sent to look at them

161
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:57,009
came back puzzled.

162
00:05:57,009 --> 00:05:59,841
The Spartans were exercising and combing

163
00:05:59,841 --> 00:06:01,102
their hair.

164
00:06:01,102 --> 00:06:03,250
A Spartan exile at the king's court,

165
00:06:03,250 --> 00:06:05,096
Demaratus, explained:

166
00:06:05,096 --> 00:06:06,733
that is what they do when they

167
00:06:06,733 --> 00:06:09,254
are about to risk their lives.

168
00:06:11,861 --> 00:06:14,113
On the fifth day Xerxes sent in

169
00:06:14,113 --> 00:06:16,040
the Medes and Cissians,

170
00:06:16,040 --> 00:06:18,516
with orders to take the Greeks alive.

171
00:06:18,516 --> 00:06:20,956
They attacked the wall for hours.

172
00:06:22,418 --> 00:06:24,716
Short spears against long ones,

173
00:06:24,716 --> 00:06:26,393
wicker against bronze,

174
00:06:26,393 --> 00:06:28,006
in a space where only the front

175
00:06:28,006 --> 00:06:29,805
of the column could fight.

176
00:06:29,805 --> 00:06:32,220
Herodotus says they made it clear to

177
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:34,496
everyone, and to the king

178
00:06:34,496 --> 00:06:37,735
above all, that he had many men

179
00:06:37,735 --> 00:06:39,397
but few soldiers.

180
00:06:40,805 --> 00:06:41,734
Then

181
00:06:41,734 --> 00:06:43,535
he sent the Immortals.

182
00:06:43,928 --> 00:06:45,774
They did no better.

183
00:06:45,774 --> 00:06:48,317
The Spartans, fighting in front,

184
00:06:48,317 --> 00:06:50,193
would turn and feign flight,

185
00:06:50,193 --> 00:06:52,422
draw the pursuers into a rush,

186
00:06:52,422 --> 00:06:54,721
then wheel and cut them down.

187
00:06:54,721 --> 00:06:56,358
Three times that day,

188
00:06:56,358 --> 00:06:58,807
Herodotus says, the king leapt up from

189
00:06:58,807 --> 00:07:01,085
his seat in fear for his army.

190
00:07:01,085 --> 00:07:02,393
Before the battle,

191
00:07:02,393 --> 00:07:04,895
a Spartan named Dienekes was told that

192
00:07:04,895 --> 00:07:08,243
Persian arrows would hide the sun.

193
00:07:08,509 --> 00:07:10,588
Good, he said:

194
00:07:10,588 --> 00:07:13,294
then we will fight in the shade.

195
00:07:15,233 --> 00:07:17,868
The second day went the same way.

196
00:07:17,868 --> 00:07:20,086
The Persians attacked in relays;

197
00:07:20,086 --> 00:07:22,176
the Greeks rotated city by city,

198
00:07:22,176 --> 00:07:24,725
so fresh men always held the line.

199
00:07:24,725 --> 00:07:26,780
And then, that evening,

200
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:28,487
a local man came to the king's

201
00:07:28,487 --> 00:07:29,195
tent.

202
00:07:29,195 --> 00:07:31,378
His name was Ephialtes,

203
00:07:31,378 --> 00:07:32,923
from Trachis.

204
00:07:34,949 --> 00:07:36,586
He knew a path.

205
00:07:36,586 --> 00:07:38,200
The Anopaea:

206
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:40,081
a mountain track that climbed behind the

207
00:07:40,081 --> 00:07:41,938
pass, over the ridge,

208
00:07:41,938 --> 00:07:45,274
and came down beyond the Greek wall.

209
00:07:45,274 --> 00:07:47,387
Leonidas knew it too.

210
00:07:47,387 --> 00:07:49,604
He had posted the thousand Phocians

211
00:07:49,604 --> 00:07:50,895
there to guard it.

212
00:07:51,197 --> 00:07:52,881
At lamp-lighting time,

213
00:07:52,881 --> 00:07:55,493
Hydarnes led the Immortals out of the

214
00:07:55,493 --> 00:07:58,545
camp and up into the oak forest.

215
00:07:58,545 --> 00:08:00,683
They marched all night.

216
00:08:02,578 --> 00:08:04,807
At first light the Phocians heard

217
00:08:04,807 --> 00:08:06,885
footsteps on fallen leaves,

218
00:08:06,885 --> 00:08:08,963
and looked up at thousands of men

219
00:08:08,963 --> 00:08:10,834
already on top of them.

220
00:08:12,874 --> 00:08:14,687
Arrows came down.

221
00:08:14,999 --> 00:08:17,692
The Phocians pulled back to a hilltop

222
00:08:17,692 --> 00:08:19,157
to make a stand.

223
00:08:19,578 --> 00:08:21,934
The Persians ignored them and kept

224
00:08:21,934 --> 00:08:25,500
moving, down toward the pass.

225
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,321
Before dawn, lookouts came running down

226
00:08:28,321 --> 00:08:29,355
the mountain.

227
00:08:29,355 --> 00:08:30,819
The Greeks knew.

228
00:08:31,802 --> 00:08:33,636
They held a council.

229
00:08:33,636 --> 00:08:35,064
What was said,

230
00:08:35,064 --> 00:08:36,680
nobody recorded.

231
00:08:38,299 --> 00:08:41,434
Herodotus says Leonidas sent the allies

232
00:08:41,434 --> 00:08:44,279
away and kept the Spartans.

233
00:08:44,279 --> 00:08:46,311
The seven hundred Thespians,

234
00:08:46,311 --> 00:08:49,830
under Demophilus, refused to leave.

235
00:08:51,064 --> 00:08:54,066
The four hundred Thebans also stayed.

236
00:08:54,066 --> 00:08:55,368
Why stay?

237
00:08:55,668 --> 00:08:57,967
Herodotus points to an oracle:

238
00:08:57,967 --> 00:08:59,813
Sparta would lose a king,

239
00:08:59,813 --> 00:09:01,673
or lose the city.

240
00:09:01,673 --> 00:09:04,018
Modern historians point at the road:

241
00:09:04,018 --> 00:09:05,899
somebody had to hold the pass long

242
00:09:05,899 --> 00:09:08,256
enough for the rest to get clear,

243
00:09:08,256 --> 00:09:10,996
and for the fleet at Artemisium to

244
00:09:10,996 --> 00:09:12,467
learn what had happened.

245
00:09:12,467 --> 00:09:15,010
At mid-morning, Xerxes attacked from the

246
00:09:15,010 --> 00:09:15,749
front.

247
00:09:15,749 --> 00:09:17,828
This time the Greeks did not wait

248
00:09:17,828 --> 00:09:18,926
behind the wall.

249
00:09:18,926 --> 00:09:20,818
They came out into the wider part

250
00:09:20,818 --> 00:09:21,747
of the pass,

251
00:09:21,747 --> 00:09:23,210
where more of them could fight at

252
00:09:23,210 --> 00:09:25,404
once, knowing the men on the path

253
00:09:25,404 --> 00:09:27,554
would soon arrive behind them.

254
00:09:28,401 --> 00:09:30,306
The spears broke.

255
00:09:30,306 --> 00:09:32,178
They fought with swords.

256
00:09:33,298 --> 00:09:35,576
Leonidas fell.

257
00:09:37,429 --> 00:09:39,705
Four times the two sides fought over

258
00:09:39,705 --> 00:09:42,514
his body, and four times the Greeks

259
00:09:42,514 --> 00:09:44,209
dragged it back.

260
00:09:44,209 --> 00:09:45,683
Then the Immortals came down the

261
00:09:45,683 --> 00:09:47,467
mountain into the Greeks' rear.

262
00:09:47,467 --> 00:09:49,580
The survivors withdrew behind the wall

263
00:09:49,580 --> 00:09:51,821
to a low mound and formed up

264
00:09:51,821 --> 00:09:53,539
on it, with swords,

265
00:09:53,539 --> 00:09:55,687
with hands, with teeth,

266
00:09:55,687 --> 00:09:57,372
Herodotus says.

267
00:09:58,954 --> 00:10:01,067
The Persians did not close with them.

268
00:10:01,067 --> 00:10:03,774
They buried the hill in arrows.

269
00:10:04,041 --> 00:10:07,199
In 1939, a Greek archaeologist,

270
00:10:07,199 --> 00:10:11,020
Spyridon Marinatos, dug into that mound.

271
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:12,831
He found hundreds of Persian bronze

272
00:10:12,831 --> 00:10:13,936
arrowheads.

273
00:10:15,941 --> 00:10:17,555
According to Herodotus,

274
00:10:17,555 --> 00:10:19,076
the Thebans surrendered and were

275
00:10:19,076 --> 00:10:20,040
branded.

276
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:23,209
The Thespians died with the Spartans.

277
00:10:23,209 --> 00:10:25,916
Their names are rarely remembered.

278
00:10:26,231 --> 00:10:29,505
Xerxes ordered Leonidas's head cut off

279
00:10:29,505 --> 00:10:31,339
and his body impaled,

280
00:10:31,339 --> 00:10:33,534
which Herodotus notes was unlike the

281
00:10:33,534 --> 00:10:36,181
Persians, who usually honoured brave

282
00:10:36,181 --> 00:10:37,800
enemies.

283
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,576
The same three days,

284
00:10:39,576 --> 00:10:40,911
the fleets had been fighting off

285
00:10:40,911 --> 00:10:44,234
Artemisium, in sight of the pass.

286
00:10:45,183 --> 00:10:47,621
A storm had already wrecked many Persian

287
00:10:47,621 --> 00:10:48,550
ships.

288
00:10:48,550 --> 00:10:50,267
The battle was a draw.

289
00:10:50,267 --> 00:10:52,879
When the news came from Thermopylae,

290
00:10:52,879 --> 00:10:55,458
the Greek fleet withdrew in the night.

291
00:10:55,458 --> 00:10:57,770
The Persians marched south.

292
00:10:58,035 --> 00:10:59,765
Athens emptied its people onto the

293
00:10:59,765 --> 00:11:03,240
islands, and the Acropolis burned.

294
00:11:04,582 --> 00:11:07,658
Then, in the narrow strait of Salamis,

295
00:11:07,658 --> 00:11:11,269
the Greek fleet destroyed Xerxes's.

296
00:11:11,269 --> 00:11:12,850
The king went home.

297
00:11:13,228 --> 00:11:15,132
The army he left behind was broken

298
00:11:15,132 --> 00:11:17,326
the next summer at Plataea,

299
00:11:17,326 --> 00:11:19,149
by a Greek line with the Spartans

300
00:11:19,149 --> 00:11:20,289
at its heart.

301
00:11:20,586 --> 00:11:23,883
So, did Thermopylae matter?

302
00:11:23,883 --> 00:11:26,403
Three days did not stop the invasion.

303
00:11:26,403 --> 00:11:28,227
The pass fell.

304
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:31,793
But the retreat was orderly,

305
00:11:31,793 --> 00:11:33,395
the fleet survived,

306
00:11:33,395 --> 00:11:36,158
and every city in Greece now knew

307
00:11:36,158 --> 00:11:38,667
what resistance looked like.

308
00:11:38,667 --> 00:11:41,175
Simonides wrote the lines later carved

309
00:11:41,175 --> 00:11:42,220
at the pass:

310
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:44,588
"Stranger, go tell the Spartans that

311
00:11:44,588 --> 00:11:46,968
here, obedient to their laws,

312
00:11:46,968 --> 00:11:48,235
we lie."

313
00:11:50,809 --> 00:11:52,075
The famous answer,

314
00:11:52,075 --> 00:11:53,700
"Come and take them",

315
00:11:53,700 --> 00:11:55,744
does not appear in Herodotus.

316
00:11:55,744 --> 00:11:57,671
It was written down by Plutarch,

317
00:11:57,671 --> 00:11:59,229
five centuries later.

318
00:11:59,531 --> 00:12:01,691
About forty years after the battle,

319
00:12:01,691 --> 00:12:03,839
Leonidas's bones were carried home to

320
00:12:03,839 --> 00:12:06,114
Sparta, and games were held there in

321
00:12:06,114 --> 00:12:07,938
his name every year.

322
00:12:07,938 --> 00:12:09,425
The sea has gone.

323
00:12:09,425 --> 00:12:11,029
The gates are quiet.

324
00:12:12,416 --> 00:12:13,786
But the low hill is still where

325
00:12:13,786 --> 00:12:16,178
it was, and the arrowheads were still

326
00:12:16,178 --> 00:12:17,120
in it.

327
00:12:20,067 --> 00:12:21,600
If this stayed with you,

328
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:24,885
subscribe and turn on notifications:

329
00:12:24,885 --> 00:12:27,474
the next documentary is already on its

330
00:12:27,474 --> 00:12:28,544
way.
